[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce the outage on PC11. End-to-end tests show normal connectivity:
- PC11 (172.16.20.11/24) can ping neighbors in VLAN 20 (pc9, pc10) and reach other subnets (e.g., 172.16.10.5) with 0% loss and low RTT.
- Other hosts can ping PC11 successfully, confirming bidirectional reachability.
- On PC11, eth0 is UP with link detected, full duplex, no errors reported; IP config is correct with a default route via 172.16.20.254. The default gateway responds, and ARP neighbors are present.

Mechanism: Because PC11 can ARP for peers and the gateway, exchange ICMP within its VLAN and across VLANs, and is reachable from the rest of the network, neither L2 (VLAN 20 on switch2 port for PC11) nor L3 are impaired. The symptom “can’t reach anything” is not currently observable.

Localization: No fault localized. PC11’s NIC, its access port (switch2.eth5 → access_vlan20_port6), and the inter-VLAN routing path are behaving normally.

Root cause: The earlier loss of connectivity was likely transient (e.g., brief link flap, host network stack hiccup) and has cleared.

Corrective fix: No corrective action required now. Advise the user to retest. If the issue recurs, capture the time and: 1) bounce the PC11 NIC or reseat the patch lead, 2) check switch2 port access_vlan20_port6 for errors/err-disable, and 3) review PC11 system logs for NIC resets. Continuous monitoring is recommended to catch any intermittent events. 
=== END ===